---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — ArNega"
  description="How ArNega handles your screen: captures are processed in memory and sent only to local Ollama. No accounts, no telemetry, no analytics."
  path="privacy"
>
  <Nav home={false} />
  <main class="container prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Applies to ArNega for macOS v0.1.x and this website.</p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      ArNega is built so that your screen content stays on your Mac. Screen analysis is performed
      by a model running locally through Ollama. ArNega has no accounts, no telemetry, no
      analytics, and no cloud AI integration.
    </p>

    <h2>Screen captures</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A capture happens only when you trigger it (pressing Enter while ArNega is focused).
      </li>
      <li>
        The primary capture path processes the image entirely in memory: capture → optimize → send
        to Ollama at <code>127.0.0.1:11434</code> → discard. Captures are not saved to disk by
        this path.
      </li>
      <li>
        If the primary capture method fails, a fallback uses the macOS
        <code>screencapture</code> utility, which briefly writes a temporary file that ArNega
        deletes immediately after reading it.
      </li>
      <li>Answers are kept in memory for the current session only and are not persisted.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What ArNega stores locally</h2>
    <p>ArNega creates exactly one file of its own:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>~/Library/Application Support/ArNega/settings.json</code> — your preferences (model
        choice, answer style, shortcut, overlay position). “Clear local state” in Settings deletes
        it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Like every macOS app with a web-based interface, the system may also keep standard cache
      files inside the same folder. None of them contain your captures or answers.
    </p>

    <h2>Network access</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        All AI inference traffic goes to the local Ollama server on your machine
        (<code>127.0.0.1:11434</code>) — never to a cloud AI endpoint.
      </li>
      <li>
        The only external connections are ones you explicitly trigger, such as clicking the link
        to Ollama’s download page, and the model download that you approve, which Ollama itself
        performs from its own registry.
      </li>
      <li>ArNega contains no update pings, crash reporting, or telemetry of any kind.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Static pages, served from GitHub Pages. No analytics, no tracking pixels, no cookies.</li>
      <li>No cookie banner is shown because there is nothing to consent to.</li>
      <li>
        Downloads are served by GitHub Releases; GitHub’s own
        <a class="link-accent" href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>
        applies to requests made to their servers.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we can’t promise</h2>
    <p>
      ArNega runs on top of macOS, Electron, and Ollama. Those platforms have their own behavior —
      for example, the operating system may perform its own network activity, and Ollama manages
      its model files under <code>~/.ollama</code>. ArNega’s promise is precise: its own
      application logic never sends your screen content anywhere except local Ollama.
    </p>
  </main>
  <Footer home={false} />
</Base>
